.TH APKG 1 "October 2026" "apkg 0.3.4" "Amix User Commands"
.SH NAME
apkg \- remote package client for Amiga Unix (SVR4)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B apkg
[
.B \-n
] [
.B \-f
] [
.B \-v
] [
.B \-c
.I conffile
] [
.B \-R
.I root
] [
.B \-y
|
.B \-\-conf\-keep
|
.B \-\-conf\-new
] [
.B \-\-force\-remove\-essential
]
.I verb
[
.I args
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B apkg
fetches SVR4 datastream packages from a remote repository over plain
HTTP and drives the local
.BR pkgadd (1M)
and
.BR pkgrm (1M)
to install and remove them.  Installed state is read from the stock
.B /var/sadm/pkg
database, so
.B apkg
coexists with the native tools.
.SH VERBS
.TP
.B update
Fetch
.IB repo /catalog
into
.B /var/apkg/catalog
(temporary file plus rename).  When the repository also publishes
.BR catalog.md5 ,
the download is verified against it; a missing (404)
.B catalog.md5
is not an error.  A catalog whose first line is not
.B "#apkg-catalog 1"
is rejected.
.TP
.B list \fR[\fBinstalled\fR|\fBall\fR]
List catalog packages, one per line: name, catalog version, an
installed marker with the installed version (or
.B (none)
for VERSION-less stock packages), category and description.  An
installed essential package shows as
.B "[installed \fIversion\fB, essential]"
(see
.BR "ESSENTIAL PACKAGES" ).
.B installed
restricts the list to packages present in
.BR /var/sadm/pkg ,
and to packages removed with edited config files kept, which show as
.B [config-files]
(see
.BR "CONFIG FILES" ).
.TP
.BI search " substr"
Case-insensitive substring match against package names and
descriptions.
.TP
.BI info " name"
Print the full catalog entry and installed state of one package, with
an
.B Essential:
line when the installed package is essential.
.TP
.BI download " name"
Fetch the package file into the cache directory and verify its md5
checksum against the catalog.
.TP
.BI install " name ..."
Resolve dependencies recursively from the catalog, download and
verify every needed package first, then run
.B pkgadd \-n \-a
.I admin
.B \-d
.I file pkginst
for each in dependency order.  Packages already installed at the same
version are skipped unless
.B \-f
is given.  A package that is installed at an
.I older
version than the catalog's is not silently skipped: if it was named on
the command line,
.B install
refuses and points at
.BR upgrade ;
if it only arrived as somebody's dependency, the version gap is
reported, the dependency counts as satisfied, and nothing is upgraded
behind your back.  Filenames ending in
.B .Z
are decompressed with
.BR zcat (1)
before being handed to
.BR pkgadd .
A package that names the filesystems it needs (see
.B FILESYSTEM
below) and would land on another is refused before anything is
installed.
.TP
.BI upgrade " name" ...
For each named package whose catalog version is newer than the installed
one, download and verify the new package and then run a single
.B pkgadd
over the installed instance, with an
.B instance=overwrite
.BR admin (4)
file.  There is no
.B pkgrm
step: nothing is removed before its replacement is in place, so a
failed fetch, a failed checksum or a failed
.B pkgadd
all leave the installed package exactly where it was.  (The earlier
pkgrm-then-pkgadd sequence could, and on real hardware did, leave the
machine with the package uninstalled and unreplaceable.)  An installed
package without a VERSION field counts as older than any catalog
version.  When the installed version is equal or newer, say so and
leave that name out (exit 0 when nothing is left).
.IP
Several names are ONE transaction: every package is fetched and verified
before the first
.BR pkgadd ,
one journal covers them, and a transaction that changes kernel space ends
with one kbootset build and commit (so a kernel, the loader and kbootset
upgrade into one generation).  A name not in the catalog (exit 5) or not
installed (exit 1) refuses the whole call before anything is fetched.  No
dependency is pulled in: only the named packages move.
.TP
.BR remove " [" \-\-purge ]
.I name
Wrapper around
.B pkgrm \-n \-a
.IR admin .
Config files you edited are kept; with
.B \-\-purge
it is
.BR purge .
The name is mapped to its package instance through the catalog when
one is available; otherwise it is used as the instance name directly.
A kernel-space package is known by its record (see
.BR "KERNEL SPACE" );
it is removed with
.B pkgrm
of the instance owning its recorded files, its record is deleted, and
the next generation is built and committed without it.
An essential package is refused (see
.BR "ESSENTIAL PACKAGES" ).
.TP
.BI purge " name"
Remove the package if it is installed, then delete the config files
.B remove
keeps: your edited files, their
.B .new
and
.B .old
files, their
.B PENDING
lines and the package's baselines.  A package already removed with its
config files kept
.RB ( [config-files] )
is purged without a pkgrm.  An installed essential package is refused,
as by
.BR remove .
.TP
.BR conffiles " [" list | resolve "] [\fIname\fR]"
.B list
(the default) shows the config files of every package, or of
.IR name ,
that wait with a new default beside them
.RB ( pending )
or that you have edited
.RB ( edited ),
also for packages already removed.
.B resolve
decides the pending ones: at a terminal it asks file by file, as after
an upgrade; with
.B \-\-conf\-keep
or
.B \-\-conf\-new
it decides without asking.  Without a terminal and without either flag it
refuses (exit 1).
.TP
.B recover
Finish a transaction that was interrupted (a power cut, a killed
process) or that ended owing work, from the journal
.BR /var/apkg/txn :
every package not yet done is installed again from its staged
datastream (re-checked against its size and catalog hash first) with
.BR instance=overwrite ,
or removed (a remove, or the other kernel package of a line that an
install replaces first; see
.BR "KERNEL SPACE" ),
and a kernel-space transaction ends with the kbootset
build and commit.  With
.B \-f
the journal is printed and discarded and nothing else is changed; when
that leaves a line without a kernel package (a replacement cut between
its removal and the new install) it says so.
While a journal exists,
.BR install ,
.B upgrade
and
.B remove
refuse to start.
.TP
.B version
Print the client version.
.SH FILESYSTEM
A package whose
.B pkginfo
carries
.B APKG_FSTYPE=ufs
(a comma-separated list of filesystem types as
.BR statvfs (2)
names them) installs only onto those filesystems.  Packages with file
names longer than 14 bytes carry it, because s5 cuts every name at 14
bytes.  Before the first
.B pkgadd
of an
.B install
or
.BR upgrade ,
apkg checks the directory of every such long name (or, when the
package has none, its install base), under the
.B \-R
target, by its nearest existing directory, and refuses the whole
transaction with exit 5 when one is on another filesystem type:
.PP
.RS
.nf
apkg: perl: refused: it installs only on ufs, and
/opt/amix/lib/perl5 is on s5, which cuts file names at 14 bytes
.fi
.RE
.PP
(one line).  A malformed value refuses the package with exit 3.
.B \-f
does not override the check; an unmarked package is never checked.
.SH "ESSENTIAL PACKAGES"
A package the system cannot boot or run without, such as the base
package that owns
.B /etc/inittab
and
.BR /etc/default/login ,
carries
.PP
.RS
.B APKG_ESSENTIAL=yes
.RE
.PP
in its
.BR pkginfo (5).
Removing such a package would delete its files, and its
.BR conffile (5)
class script deletes every config file still equal to its default.
.B remove
and
.B purge
(also with
.BR \-n )
refuse an installed essential package before the journal or
.B pkgrm
is touched, with exit 5:
.PP
.RS
.nf
apkg: base (pkginst BASE) is essential (APKG_ESSENTIAL=yes): the
system may not boot without it; not removed
(\-\-force\-remove\-essential overrides)
.fi
.RE
.PP
(one line).  The mark is read from the installed
.BI /var/sadm/pkg/ pkginst /pkginfo\fR,
so it is the installed version that decides.
.B no
or no line at all means not essential; any other value counts as
.BR yes .
.B \-f
does not override the refusal;
.B \-\-force\-remove\-essential
does, with a warning.  Installing over the package, and the swap of
one kernel package of a line for another (see
.BR "KERNEL SPACE" ),
are not removals and are not affected.
.PP
Only
.B apkg
honours the mark.  The engine ignores the parameter, and the stock
.BR pkgrm (1M)
removes an essential package like any other when asked to directly.
.SH KERNEL SPACE
Kernel, loader and module packages are listed in a block of the
catalog that every older client skips as comments (kernel/dlm contract
C2-2, version 5):
.PP
.RS
.nf
#ext kpkg 1
#kpkg <name> <version> role=kernel|module line=k30|k46
      kabi=<tag> pin=<compiler pin> size=<bytes> sha256=<64 hex>
      file=<datastream>
#kpkg <name> <version> role=loader line=any loader=<n>
      size=<bytes> sha256=<64 hex> file=<datastream>
#ksig <key-id> <file>
#end kpkg
.fi
.RE
.PP
(one
.B #kpkg
line per package, every line under 2048 bytes; optional
.B pkginst=
and
.BR offset= ).
The keys depend on the role: an entry that lacks a key its role
requires, or carries one its role excludes
.RB ( kabi=
or
.B pin=
on a loader), is refused.  An unknown key is
ignored; an unknown key starting with
.B !
refuses that entry.  A structural error (an unterminated block, an
ordinary entry inside it, a second block, a name used twice) rejects
the catalog.  A block of another version is skipped.
.PP
A kernel-space package is verified by
.B SHA-256
and size after download (userland packages stay md5 and size).
There is no signature in this release: a
.B #ksig
line is recorded and not acted on, the catalog block is reported
"unverified", and every record says
.BR "verified no" .
.PP
Before anything is fetched, a transaction is pre-flighted: a module
must match the kernel of its line (in the transaction, or installed)
in kabi family and major, its minor must not exceed the kernel's, and
the compiler pins must be equal; a static kernel takes no modules; a
new kernel may not strand an installed module of its line.  A refusal
exits 5 and changes nothing.  The box's CPU line is checked by
kbootset (its gate 10), not here.
.PP
.B One kernel package per line
(C2-2 version 5 \(sc2.1).  Every kernel package of a line, a static
family's and the module kernel's alike, installs
.BI /stand/ line /unix
(and its kabi sidecar).  So when a transaction installs a kernel and a
kernel record of the same line exists under another package instance,
apkg replaces that package in the same journaled transaction.  The plan
(and
.BR \-n )
says so:
.PP
.RS
.nf
kernel-k46-m replaces kernel-k46-z (KERN46Z): one kernel package per line
.fi
.RE
.PP
The pre-flight then checks the transaction and every installed module
against the new kernel; a module that does not fit refuses the
transaction before anything changes ("zeth46: built for kabi k46.1.0,
the new kernel kernel-k46-z is static: remove the module packages
first"), so going back from the module kernel to a static family needs
the module packages removed first.  Once everything is staged and
verified, the other package is removed
.RB ( pkgrm ,
then its record; "removed KERN46Z") before the new
.BR pkgadd ,
because both ship the same files; then the new package is installed and
recorded, and the kbootset trigger runs once at the end.  A cut between
the removal and the install leaves the line without a kernel package
until
.B apkg recover
finishes the install; the box still boots meanwhile, from the boot
store's own copies of the kernel.  Two or more kernel records of one
line (a box damaged by hand) refuse any kernel or module install of
that line ("two kernel records for line k46 (KERN46M, KERN46Z): remove
one"), as do two kernels of one line in the same transaction.  A
package of the same instance is an upgrade, as before.
.PP
A kernel-space package is pure files: once staged, and before anything
is installed, its pkgmap may hold only
.B f
and
.B d
objects of class
.B none
and no install scripts (of the
.B i
entries only pkginfo, depend and copyright), and at least one file.
A module package may also carry the device nodes of its driver's majors
(C3-5 version 4) as
.B c
entries of class
.B none
under
.BR /dev/ ,
in full
.RB ( "c none /dev/zen0 51 0 0644 root root" );
pkgadd makes the node and records it in the contents database.
Anything else
.RB ( e ,
.BR v ,
links, block devices, a character device in a kernel or loader package
or outside
.BR /dev/ ,
another class, a script) refuses the transaction with
exit 3.
.PP
After each kernel-space
.BR pkgadd ,
apkg writes the record
.BI /var/apkg/kpkg/ pkginst
\(em the file is named by the entry's
.B pkginst=
(else its name), because an s5 root keeps 14 characters of a file name
and a long name and its
.B .new
would collide; the full name is the record's
.B name
line (C2-2 version 4) \(em
(name, version, role, line, then kabi and pin for a kernel or module or
loader for the loader, the datastream's sha256, the
source, the install time,
.BR "verified no" ,
and one
.BI "file " "path size sha256"
line per installed plain file, taken from the contents database and hashed
on disk; a module's device node has no bytes and no
.B file
line: the contents database records it, and pkgrm removes it).  The instance pkgadd installs comes from the datastream's header and
must equal
.B pkginst=
when the entry gives one.
.PP
After a transaction that installed or removed kernel space, and only
when every package in it is done, apkg calls
.B kbootset build
and then
.B kbootset commit
(two calls, with
.B \-R
.I target
under
.B \-R
and an explicit
.BR \-\-journal ).
apkg never writes a boot slice itself.  With
.B bootset=manual
in
.B /etc/default/bootset
it prints the two commands instead.  On success it repeats kbootset's
last line ("generation N committed; power-cycle to boot it").  A refusal
(kbootset status 3 or 4) exits 7 with kbootset's line and "the committed
generation is unchanged"; an error (status 1 or 2), or a kbootset that
cannot run, exits 6 and keeps the journal, so
.B apkg recover
runs the build and commit again.  A failure after a good build leaves
the built generation uncommitted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-n
Dry run: print what would be fetched and which commands would run,
change nothing.
.TP
.B \-f
Force: reinstall the packages
.I named on the command line
even when they are already installed.  It does
.B not
propagate to their dependencies \- an already-satisfied dependency is
left alone (forcing the whole closure meant re-installing the multi-
megabyte engine package on every run, minutes of pointless work on a
68k).  A dependency that is genuinely missing is still installed.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose: progress notes, HTTP details, exact command lines.
.TP
.BI \-c " conffile"
Read configuration from
.I conffile
instead of
.BR /etc/apkg.conf .
.TP
.BI \-R " root"
Install into, remove from and read the state of the system under
.I root
(the engine runs with
.BR "\-R \fIroot\fR" );
see
.BR FILESYSTEM .
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-conf\-keep
Keep every edited config file without asking, and delete the new
default's
.B .new
file (see
.BR "CONFIG FILES" ).
For batch runs and the installer.
.TP
.B \-\-conf\-new
Take every new config default without asking; your file is saved as
.IB file .old\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-force\-remove\-essential
Let
.B remove
and
.B purge
remove an essential package (see
.BR "ESSENTIAL PACKAGES" ).
Like the other long options it comes before the verb.
.SH "CONFIG FILES"
A package may mark files you edit in place, such as
.BR /etc/apkg.conf ,
as config files
.RB ( conffile (5)).
The package's own scripts keep an edited one in every
.B pkgadd
and write the new default beside it as
.IB file .new\fR;
the file is then listed in
.BR /var/sadm/conffiles/PENDING .
The package tools cannot ask anything while they run, so
.B apkg
asks afterwards.  After an
.B install
or
.B upgrade
it lists the pending files of the packages it installed and then, per
file:
.TP 4
\(bu
at a terminal, with no flag, asks:
.BR k eep
yours (the default answer),
install the
.BR n ew
default, show the
.BR d iff
of yours against it, or decide
.BR l ater;
.TP
\(bu
with
.B \-y
or
.BR \-\-conf\-keep ,
keeps yours;
.TP
\(bu
with
.BR \-\-conf\-new ,
installs the new default;
.TP
\(bu
otherwise (no terminal, no flag) changes nothing: the file stays pending
for
.BR "apkg conffiles resolve" .
.PP
Keeping yours deletes the
.B .new
file; the default stays in the package's baseline under
.BR /var/sadm/conffiles .
Taking the new default renames yours to
.IB file .old\fR,
moves the
.B .new
file into place and records it in the package database with
.BR "installf \-c conffile" .
A file you have edited is asked about only when the package's default
changed.
.B remove
keeps the files you edited;
.B purge
deletes them.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B /etc/apkg.conf
holds
.B key=value
lines;
.B #
starts a comment
.RB ( apkg.conf (5)).
It is a
.BR conffile (5)
of the APKG package: an upgrade installs the new default over a file you
never edited, and keeps one you did, writing the new default beside it as
.BR /etc/apkg.conf.new .
The default of the installed version is also
.BR /usr/apkg/lib/apkg.conf .
A key the file does not name takes its built-in default.  Keys and defaults:
.TP
.B repo
Repository base URL (default
.BR http://pkg.amigaux.org ).
The host is a name-based virtual host, so the repository must be named by
hostname; a bare IP address is answered 404.  Stock Amix has no resolver, so
either add the address to
.B /etc/inet/hosts
or configure
.B nameserver
below (see
.BR "NAME RESOLUTION" ).
The catalog is fetched from
.IB repo /catalog
and packages from
.IB repo /pkgs/ filename\fR.
.TP
.B cachedir
Package download cache (default
.BR /var/apkg/cache ).
The catalog lives next to it, in the cache directory's parent.
.TP
.B pkgadd
pkgadd binary (default
.BR /usr/apkg/bin/pkgadd ;
falls back to
.B /usr/sbin/pkgadd
when the default is missing).
.TP
.B pkgrm
pkgrm binary (default
.BR /usr/apkg/bin/pkgrm ,
fallback
.BR /usr/sbin/pkgrm ).
.TP
.B admin
.BR admin (4)
file passed to pkgadd/pkgrm with
.B \-a
(default
.BR /var/apkg/admin ).
.B apkg
creates this file if it is missing \- it is shipped by the APKG package
itself, so a
.B pkgrm APKG
deletes it if you never edited it, and an engine call that merely assumed
it was there would fail with
.RB \(lq "unable to open admin file" \(rq.
An existing file is never rewritten, and it is a
.BR conffile (5)
like
.BR /etc/apkg.conf ,
so an upgrade of APKG keeps your edits (see
.BR admin (5)).  For an
.B upgrade
(and for a
.B \-f
install over a different version)
.B apkg
derives a second file,
.IR admin.overwrite ,
from it: every key you set is carried across and only
.B instance=
is replaced with
.BR overwrite .
.PP
The remaining keys tune the fetch path.  Every constant that path uses
is one of these \- there is no compiled-in number an operator cannot
move.
.TP
.B kbootset
The generation composer called after a kernel-space transaction;
default
.BR /sbin/kbootset .
Like pkgadd it runs from this system and is told
.B \-R
.I target
under
.BR \-R .
.TP
.B kbootset_journal
The
.B \-\-journal
path handed to kbootset; empty (the default) means
.IB target /var/kbootset/journal ,
the contract's own default, passed explicitly.
.TP
.B timeout
Seconds of network
.I silence
tolerated during a transfer (default 300).  This is an
.B inactivity
timeout, not a cap on how long a transfer may take: the timer is
re-armed by every chunk that arrives, so a slow but flowing transfer
never trips it however long it runs.  A multi-megabyte package over a
period link is minutes of perfectly healthy transfer; size it against
the longest legitimate
.I gap
instead.
.TP
.B connect_timeout
Seconds allowed for host resolution plus the TCP connect (default 30).
Separate from
.B timeout
so a dead host fails in seconds rather than inheriting the patience a
mid-transfer stall needs.
.TP
.B retries
Extra attempts after a
.I network
failure (default 2; 0 disables).  A non-200 HTTP status is the server
answering, not a failure, and is never retried.
.TP
.B retry_delay
Seconds before the first retry (default 5); it doubles before each
further one.  0 retries immediately.
.TP
.B progress
Bytes between progress lines on stderr (default 262144).  0 silences
them;
.B \-v
quarters the interval.  Nothing is printed for a transfer smaller than
one interval, so ordinary runs stay quiet.
.TP
.B bufsize
Socket read buffer in bytes (default 8192).  Clamped to the range
512\-262144; a value outside it is clamped with a warning.
.TP
.B redirects
Number of 301/302 hops followed (default 1).  0 refuses to follow any.
.TP
.B rcvbuf
The TCP receive window, in bytes.
.B The default is 0 \- leave the stack's window alone \- and that is a
.BR "measured result, not caution" .
.IP
Amix's compiled-in 4096-byte window looks exactly like the thing
capping a single stream on a bandwidth-delay-bound path (throughput is
at most window divided by round-trip time).  It is not.  Two
server-side packet captures of a real 68LC060 A4000D fetching from
.B pkg.amigaux.org
over the Z3660 ethernet driver (2026\-09\-14/15) found:
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.B SO_RCVBUF
64 KB requested before
.BR connect (3N);
the box advertised win 4096 in its SYN and in all 2999 steady-state
ACKs.  The socket option does not reach this
.B tcp
module at all.
.IP \(bu
Raising the window really does work through the ioctl below \- 16384
was seen on the wire \- and it is
.BR slower :
4096 gives about 54 KB/s, 8192 about 32 KB/s, 16384 about 36\-40 KB/s,
with server-side retransmits rising from 1.0% to 3.6%.
.IP \(bu
The cause is an RX-path loss cliff at roughly 8 segments in flight
(about 10 KB outstanding): up to 7 segments per burst lose 2\-3%, 8
lose about 60%, 9 or more lose about 100%, and every loss costs a
server retransmission timeout.  The stock 4096 window admits at most 8
segments (7 x 536 + 344) and so sits exactly at the loss-free edge \-
near-optimal for this driver as it stands.
.RE
.IP
The real lever is the RX drop mechanism (the Z3660 firmware discarding
its whole backlog on overflow, and the 16.67 ms poll quantum), and it
is not in this client.
.B Do not raise this expecting speed.
.IP
Setting it above 0 is an
.I operator knob,
and it does two things:
.RS
.TP 3
1.
.B STREAMS INITQPARMS.
.B apkg
issues the privileged
.B INITQPARMS
ioctl (from
.BR <net/strioc.h> ,
.BR iqp_type " = " IQP_RQ | IQP_HIWAT )
with
.BR I_STR (7)
on a TCP stream, asking the
.B tcp
module for a receive high-water mark of
.BR rcvbuf .
This is the mechanism that actually moves the window.  It requires
.B root
\-
.B apkg install
already runs as root, and a non-root
.B apkg
falls back to mechanism 2 and says so.
.B It rewrites the tcp module's default high-water mark, not this one
.BR stream's ,
so every TCP stream opened on the box afterwards inherits it \-
box-wide, not
.B apkg
wide, and standing until reboot.
.B apkg
therefore says so at warning level, not only under
.BR \-v .
Nothing can read the previous value back (the ioctl is set-only, and
.BR sockmod 's
cached
.B SO_RCVBUF
starts at 0 rather than at the module's high-water mark), so
.B apkg
does not restore it:
.B put it back with a root run at rcvbuf=4096, or by rebooting.
The value is capped at 65535 because the ioctl carries a 16-bit field.
.TP
2.
.B SO_RCVBUF
requested with
.BR setsockopt (3N)
before
.BR connect (3N),
kept as the unprivileged fallback and for non-Amix hosts.
.B On Amix this alone does not reach the tcp module.
The
.BR getsockopt (3N)
value
.B \-v
prints can come from
.BR sockmod 's
own cached copy while TCP below it still runs at 4096; read that line
as "what was asked, and what the socket layer says", never as "what
TCP is using".
.RE
.IP
If you do set it, 16384 is the highest worth considering.  Nothing
above 65535 can be advertised in any case (Amix predates RFC 1323, so
there is no window scaling), and the practical limit is lower still:
MSS to a non-local host is pinned at 536 and the Z3660 firmware's RX
ring has 32 usable slots, so past 32 x 536, about 17 KB, the ring
itself overruns \- and that firmware discards its entire queued
backlog rather than back-pressuring, which TCP sees as a full-window
loss and a retransmission timeout.
.TP
.B sndbuf
.B SO_SNDBUF
in bytes (default 0, i.e. leave the stack default).  apkg sends
requests and receives bodies, so this has no effect on fetch
throughput; it is here because the receive side is configurable and an
asymmetric pair would be a trap.
.TP
.B nameserver
IPv4 address of a nameserver for apkg's stub resolver.
.B The default list is empty,
i.e. no DNS at all \- exactly the behaviour apkg has always had.  The key
is
.B repeatable
and the servers are tried in the order written; a line with an empty value
clears the list.  A
.BI : port
suffix is accepted after the address and exists for testing; leave it off.
A name is not accepted here \- resolving the resolver is a bootstrap
problem.  See
.B NAME RESOLUTION
below.
.TP
.B dns_timeout
Seconds to wait for one DNS query
.I attempt
(default 5).  Not per name and not per nameserver: a UDP query comes back
quickly or it is lost.
.TP
.B dns_retries
Extra attempts per nameserver (default 2).  Every configured nameserver is
asked in turn regardless, so a name nothing answers costs at most
nameservers x (dns_retries + 1) x dns_timeout seconds.  A server that
answers
.I "no such name"
has answered: it is not retried.
.SH NAME RESOLUTION
.B Stock Amix has no resolver.
The system
.BR gethostbyname (3N)
reads
.B /etc/inet/hosts
and nothing else; a
.B resolv.conf
on an Amix box is inert, because nothing reads it.  The package repository
is a
.B name-based virtual host
\- a request whose
.B Host:
header carries a bare IP address is answered 404 \- so the repository has
to be named by hostname, and on a stock box that means somebody must add a
line to
.B /etc/inet/hosts
by hand before apkg can reach it at all.
.PP
apkg therefore carries its own
.B stub resolver.
A host name is resolved in three steps:
.RS
.IP 1. 4
an IPv4 literal is used as it stands;
.IP 2. 4
the host database is consulted \- that is
.BR gethostbyname (3N),
i.e.
.B /etc/inet/hosts
on Amix;
.IP 3. 4
only if that says nothing, each configured
.B nameserver
is asked, in order.
.RE
.PP
The hosts file is consulted
.B first
on purpose: a box that resolves today cannot regress when a nameserver is
configured, and a line an operator wrote by hand can never be shadowed by
DNS.
.PP
The resolver is a stub in the strict sense: one UDP query per attempt, A
records only, recursion requested, no EDNS, no cache beyond the process, no
search list, no TCP fallback and no
.BR resolv.conf .
It follows a CNAME chain to an A record and ignores TTLs.  A reply that does
not match the query it answers \- wrong transaction id, wrong question,
malformed, or a compression pointer that does not point backwards \- is
discarded and the next attempt or nameserver is tried; a partial answer is
never used.  Root privilege is not required.
.PP
.B It resolves apkg's own lookups and nothing else.
.BR telnet (1),
.BR ftp (1)
and the rest of the box still read
.B /etc/inet/hosts
and are entirely unaffected by
.BR nameserver .
Keep that file as you need it.
.PP
.B \-v
reports which of the three steps answered, and for DNS which nameserver
did.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B APKG_ROOT
Prefix prepended to every compiled-in default path
.RB ( /etc/apkg.conf ,
.BR /var/apkg/... ,
.BR /var/sadm/pkg ,
and the pkgadd/pkgrm defaults with their fallbacks).  Lets tests and
image builds point the whole client at a fake root.  Values read from
the configuration file are never prefixed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (including "nothing to do").
.TP
.B 1
Usage or configuration error.
.TP
.B 2
Network failure (name not resolved by the hosts file or any configured
nameserver, connect, inactivity timeout with the configured retries
exhausted, HTTP error status).
.TP
.B 3
Verification failure: md5, sha256 or size mismatch, bad catalog
header, malformed catalog, a refused kernel-space entry, a datastream
whose header contradicts the catalog, a kernel-space datastream that
is not pure files, a malformed
.BR APKG_FSTYPE .
.TP
.B 4
pkgadd/pkgrm (or the zcat decompression step) failed, or installf failed
to record a new config default taken with
.BR \-\-conf\-new .
.TP
.B 5
Dependency cycle, or a dependency (or install target) missing from
the catalog; a kernel-space pre-flight refusal (kabi, pin, line); a
package refused for the filesystem it would land on (see
.BR FILESYSTEM );
the removal of an essential package refused (see
.BR "ESSENTIAL PACKAGES" ).
.TP
.B 6
kbootset could not run, failed (status 1) or rejected its arguments
(status 2).  The packages are installed; the journal is kept for
.BR "apkg recover" .
.TP
.B 7
kbootset refused the build or commit by a rule (status 3) or at a gate
(status 4); the committed generation is unchanged.
.SH FILES
.PD 0
.TP 24
.B /etc/apkg.conf
configuration; a conffile, kept by an upgrade when edited
.TP
.B /etc/apkg.conf.new
the new default, when an upgrade kept your edited file
.TP
.B /usr/apkg/lib/apkg.conf
the shipped default of the installed version (not read by apkg)
.TP
.B /var/apkg/catalog
local copy of the repository catalog
.TP
.B /var/apkg/cache
downloaded packages
.TP
.B /var/apkg/admin
permissive admin(5) file used for non-interactive pkgadd/pkgrm;
created on demand if absent; a conffile, kept by an upgrade when edited
.TP
.B /var/sadm/conffiles/PENDING
the conffiles whose new default waits in a
.B .new
file; see
.BR conffile (5)
.TP
.BI /var/sadm/conffiles/ pkginst /
the baselines: the config defaults each package last shipped
.TP
.IB file .new\fR, " " \fIfile\fB.old
a new default waiting beside your file; your file after
.B \-\-conf\-new
.TP
.B /var/apkg/admin.overwrite
derived from the above with instance=overwrite, for the
.B upgrade
verb; rewritten on every use
.TP
.B /etc/inet/hosts
the host database, consulted before any nameserver (read only, and never
written by apkg)
.TP
.B /var/sadm/pkg/*/pkginfo
installed-package state (read only)
.TP
.B /var/sadm/install/contents
the files of each installed kernel-space package (read only)
.TP
.B /var/apkg/kpkg/*
apkg's install records of kernel-space packages; kbootset reads them
.TP
.B /var/apkg/txn
the transaction journal
.TP
.B /etc/default/bootset
.B bootset=auto
(the default) or
.B manual
.PD
